<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0425">Definition <span data-tag="0425">8.5.8.9</span> <span class="named">(Dyadic Homeomorphisms)</span>.</a> Recall that a <em>dyadic rational number</em> is a real number of the form $\frac{a}{2^ n}$, where $a$ and $n$ are integers. Let $s, t \geq 0$ be dyadic rational numbers. We say that a homeomorphism $f: [0,s] \xrightarrow {\sim } [0,t]$ is <em>dyadic</em> if it satisfies the following conditions: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
The function $f$ is piecewise linear; in particular, it is differentiable away from finitely many points of the closed interval $[0,s]$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $x \in [0,s]$ is a point where $f$ is not differentiable, then $x$ is a dyadic rational number. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
For every point $x \in [0,s]$ where $f$ is differentiable, the derivative $f'(x)$ is equal to $2^{n}$ for some integer $n$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
<p> Note that the third condition implies that the homeomorphism $f$ is strictly increasing, so that $f(0) = 0$ and $f(s) = t$. </p>
</article>
